er an ethiopian  airlines flight crashed after takeoff  346 people were killed in the two  crashes  an 18-month long investigation  determined boeing failed in its design  and development of the max  and the faa failed in its oversight of  boeing and its certification of the  aircraft  it also scrutinized the close ties  between the company and the regulator  the global fleet of max eights has been  grounded since march of 2019 and boeing  is still working  to recertify those jets global news has  learned justin burke the man convicted  of killing police officers in new  brunswick in 2014  is in the hospital after a drug overdose  multiple sources have told global news  that burke overdosed last week at the  atlantic institution in  new brunswick correctional service  canada would not comment on the matter  citing privacy concerns burke killed  three rcmp  officers and injured two others he was  taken into custody after a nearly  30-hour manhunt  and he is serving a life sentence  hurricane sally slams into the  southeastern u.s  coming up the path of destruction and  what is still to come  hurricane sally made landfall early this  morning in alabama and along the florida  panhandle and the damage  is devastating punishing winds and heavy  rain brought down trees and led to  catastrophic flooding  many homes and businesses are swamped  sally has now been downgraded to a  tropical storm but it's just one of four  ominous weather systems  churning in the atlantic right now as  jackson protzko reports officials are  warning  thousands may be in the path of rising  water in the days to come  a slow-motion disaster arrived along the  gulf coast  hurricane sally is a roaring storm  moving at a snail's pace  delivering unprecedented amounts of rain  as it slowly moves inland at  walking speed while winds whip  at everything in its path we had strong  winds for a long period of time  instead of just for a few hours it was  for 12 hours  in parts of alabama and the florida  panhandle there's extensive damage to  buildings  a huge section of a bridge leading out  of pensacola collapsed after being  struck by a construction barge  in alabama this marina was decimated and  dozens of boats sank  they're just beginning to tally up the  damage we're going to try to survive  this we're going to try going through  this and we're just going to be  trying to be safe but the real story is  the catastrophic  rainfall nearly a meter in some areas  downtown pensacola is under water  and flooding is expected to spread  across several states over the coming  days  catastrophic and life-threatening flash  flooding is ongoing right now with sally  along the florida and alabama coast  sally is part of an unusually active  hurricane season  with four named storms churning in the  atlantic at the same time  one more storm and forecasters will run  out of names  they'll have to use the greek alphabet  right now we are in the peak of  hurricane season  for the atlantic and typically it ends  around november 15th or november 30th  there's no reason to expect the rest of  hurricane season to be any quieter  with an ample supply of warm ocean water  on hand  leaving residents up and down the coast  to watch the forecast  while they pick up the pieces jackson  proskow global news  washington improving weather conditions  in the pacific northwest are raising  hopes of possibly getting the upper hand  on those wildfires  in washington state and parts of oregon  but winds inland are picking up in  central oregon where fires are reaching  areas untouched by flames for decades  the lakeside tourist city of detroit  oregon has lost nearly all  its businesses and public buildings  including city hall  officials are warning the area is still  extremely dangerous and residents should  not  return home  and there's no relief in sight in  california gusting winds and low  humidity are fueling more unpredictable  fires that have already burned more than  a million hectares 